+Line grammar of a capture (owner decision §0 item 29 (1); `verdict-check.mjs` applies it after removing format characters (Unicode category Cf), normalising to NFKC and mapping every dash variant (Unicode Pd and U+2212) to `-`): (g1) a header line `<key>: <value>` at line start for `fresh context`, `rejected alternative provided`, `conflict of interest`, `severity scale`, `verdict mapping` and `verdict`, the `verdict:` line exactly once; (g2) an objection line `objection: F-P<k>-<n> | artefact: <id> | evidence: <file:line or command> | refuted when: <condition>` with exactly these four fields in this order, and no other objection id and no premise mark in the line; (g3) a premise row `- <text> -- checked: <file:line or command>` or `- <text> -- unchecked`, only inside the `premises:` section, or the single line `premises: none, no existing code or data touched`; (g4) a disposition line `disposition: <capture id>:<item> | <text>`, which names an item of another capture and carries no objection of its own (the clearing branch that reads it is built in SL-5; until then the form is accepted and nothing is recorded); (g5) a quote line, starting with `> `, for the verbatim assignment or any other quoted text, which carries no objection and no premise whatever ids or marks it contains; (g6) prose, every other line: an objection id or a premise mark (`-- checked`, `-- unchecked`, any letter case) in prose rejects the capture, other prose carries no objection, and the owner reads the capture file before every record. A capture whose form fails twice is captured with `validator: rejected` (UC-12 step 4).
